import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"BiRefNet bounded lane — the precision lane on a real pretrained vision model","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"BiRefNet bounded lane — the precision lane on a real pretrained vision model","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/birefnet-bounded-lane.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/birefnet-bounded-lane.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/birefnet-bounded-lane.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="birefnet-bounded-lane-—-the-precision-lane-on-a-real-pretrained-vision-model" tabindex="-1">BiRefNet bounded lane — the precision lane on a real pretrained vision model <a class="header-anchor" href="#birefnet-bounded-lane-—-the-precision-lane-on-a-real-pretrained-vision-model" aria-label="Permalink to “BiRefNet bounded lane — the precision lane on a real pretrained vision model”">​</a></h1><p>Question: does <code>search(..., error_budget=)</code> buy headroom on a real pretrained model where the <em>exact</em> lane was already FLOP-neutral? <code>bench/suites/bounded/bounded_e2e.py</code> delivered certified-bounded members on stories15M (1.41× vs eager at 1e-2); the <code>34607e0</code> BiRefNet trial exported the real checkpoint but found no exact-lane win. The bounded lane had never been opened on it.</p><p>Probe: <code>lab/probes/birefnet_bounded_probe.py</code> (2026-10-14, <code>feat/bounded-real-model</code> worktree, RTX 2050 3.68 GB, torch 2.14.0+cu130, <code>TORCHINDUCTOR_COMPILE_THREADS=2</code>).</p><p>Model: <strong>the real <code>ZhengPeng7/BiRefNet</code> HF checkpoint</strong> — Swin-v1-L</p><ul><li>ASPPDeformable decoder, 220.18 M params fp32 (~886 MB safetensors, snapshot <code>e2bf8e4</code>), loaded via <code>AutoModelForImageSegmentation.from_pretrained(..., trust_remote_code=True, dtype=torch.float32)</code> — transformers 5.19.0, timm 1.0.30, torchvision 0.29.1, kornia 0.8.3, einops — scratch <code>--no-deps</code> installs into the worktree venv (documented in the tool docstring). Input <code>(1,3,384,384)</code> (the swin_v1_l native resolution; window 12 divides the 96×48 token grids, including the <code>x/2</code> second backbone pass). Eager forward: ~2.8 s CPU / ~0.28 s CUDA, 1.2 GB steady-state VRAM.</li></ul><h2 id="structural-census-—-the-decoder-bn-question-task-item-3" tabindex="-1">Structural census — the decoder BN question (task item 3) <a class="header-anchor" href="#structural-census-—-the-decoder-bn-question-task-item-3" aria-label="Permalink to “Structural census — the decoder BN question (task item 3)”">​</a></h2><p>Exported <code>model.decoder</code> alone on its real captured input (forward hook): <strong>237 enodes — <code>batch_norm</code> ×35</strong>, <code>conv2d</code> ×68, <code>relu_</code> ×31, <code>mul</code> ×19, <code>sigmoid</code> ×19, <code>deform_conv2d</code> ×16, <code>upsample_bilinear2d</code> ×13, <code>reshape</code> ×10, <code>concat</code> ×9, <code>permute</code> ×5, <code>dropout</code> ×4, <code>adaptive_avg_pool2d</code> ×4, <code>add</code> ×3, <code>zeros</code> ×1.</p><p><strong>Verdict: BN is present, not LN/conv-only.</strong> The decoder (<code>BasicDecBlk</code> ×5 + ASPPDeformable ×5 + <code>gdt_convs</code>) is a conv→BN→relu graph — conv+BN fusion targets genuinely exist (43 <code>nn.BatchNorm2d</code> modules in the decoder/squeeze tree, all live in eval mode — <code>config.batch_size=4 &gt; 1</code> keeps them real). But no conv+BN fold law exists in the rule set today, so they lower as <code>batch_norm</code> nodes, not fusion sites. Side finding: <code>deform_conv2d</code><em>exports</em> into IR (16 nodes) but has <strong>no torch lowering binding</strong> — leaf blocks containing it fail lower with <code>ValueError: No torch binding for op &#39;deform_conv2d&#39;</code>. The earlier trial&#39;s &quot;exports and lowers (no missing bindings)&quot; never lowered a deform site; the gap is real.</p><h2 id="compositional-plumbing-—-what-the-run-needed-to-survive" tabindex="-1">Compositional plumbing — what the run needed to survive <a class="header-anchor" href="#compositional-plumbing-—-what-the-run-needed-to-survive" aria-label="Permalink to “Compositional plumbing — what the run needed to survive”">​</a></h2><p>Two model-shape traps had to be routed before the sweep ran:</p><ul><li><code>mul_scl_ipt=&#39;cat&#39;</code> calls <code>model.bb</code> <strong>twice per forward</strong> — once on <code>x</code>, once on <code>x/2</code>. A delivered block is <em>shape-specialized</em> for its first captured call (export bakes reshape constants), so any grafted <code>BasicLayer</code>/<code>SwinTransformerBlock</code> silently breaks the second (half-resolution) call — and the compositional e2e check only <em>records</em> the failure in <code>stats[&quot;end_to_end&quot;]</code>, it does not roll back grafts. Separately the blocks read <code>self.H</code>/<code>self.W</code> mutated by the parent per call, so the captured-input replay sees the stale half-res values and the block&#39;s own <code>assert L == H*W</code> fires (all 24 failed in the first run). Fix: <code>_block_pred</code> declines <code>BasicLayer</code> / <code>SwinTransformerBlock</code> / <code>BasicDecBlk</code> / <code>_ASPPModuleDeformable</code>, so selection descends to the shape-generic leaves — and <code>_LeafComposer</code> restricts grafting to <code>nn.Linear</code> (the only leaf class the bounded specials scan: <code>offer_weight_specials</code> is 2-D <code>linear</code>/<code>matmul</code> only).</li><li>Host-RAM: <code>capture_inputs</code> retains a detached clone of every selected block&#39;s input plus a live reference to its output — ~6 GB on the full 498-block selection at 384², which OOM-killed the first sweep (the same mechanism, smaller, is why <code>bounded_e2e</code> survives on a 15 M model). The Linear-only selection needs ~0.5 GB.</li></ul><p>Sweep: <code>Optimizer(backend=TorchBackend(composer=_LeafComposer()))</code> → <code>optimize(search_model_cpu, x, strategy=Compositional(block_pred= ...)</code>, <code>detect_specials=True</code>, <code>error_budget=B)</code> — search on CPU, delivered module re-verified and timed on CUDA. 99 Linear blocks selected, all 99 optimized every cell (structural cache 80 hits / 19 misses). Timing: <code>_time_module</code> median of 30 reps under <code>no_grad</code>; inductor baseline compiled once (10.5 s) and timed once. Evidence JSON: <code>bench/results/birefnet_bounded.json</code>, <code>birefnet_bounded_3e-2.json</code> (untracked).</p><h2 id="per-budget-results" tabindex="-1">Per-budget results <a class="header-anchor" href="#per-budget-results" aria-label="Permalink to “Per-budget results”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>budget</th><th>opt s</th><th>bounded offers</th><th>delivered</th><th>bound max</th><th>fresh rel</th><th>mask IoU</th><th>mlopt µs</th><th>vs eager</th><th>vs inductor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>none</td><td>38.6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>277 948</td><td>0.999×</td><td>0.862×</td></tr><tr><td>1e-4</td><td>205.6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>277 005</td><td>0.997×</td><td>0.865×</td></tr><tr><td>1e-3</td><td>211.2</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>276 043</td><td>0.998×</td><td>0.868×</td></tr><tr><td>3e-3</td><td>221.9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>277 953</td><td>0.997×</td><td>0.862×</td></tr><tr><td>1e-2</td><td>229.2</td><td>20</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>279 075</td><td>0.996×</td><td>0.859×</td></tr><tr><td>3e-2</td><td>364.1</td><td>56</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.0000</td><td>282 116</td><td>0.993×</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>1e-1</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>OOM-killed mid-cell (dedup clustering host RAM; another agent was resident)</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Baselines: eager 277 551 µs (peak 2 032 MB), inductor 239 660 µs (peak 1 974 MB) — compiled source is already <strong>1.16× faster</strong> than eager on this model.</p><p>Offer frontier (all <code>elide_bounded</code>, <code>bound_norm=max_abs</code>):</p><ul><li><strong>1e-2</strong> — 20 offers, exactly the <code>attn.qkv</code> projections of stages 2–3 (bounds 7.75e-3 stage 2, 9.95e-3 stage 3).</li><li><strong>3e-2</strong> — 56 offers: the same 20 qkv plus <code>mlp.fc1</code> and <code>mlp.fc2</code> of all 18 stage-3 blocks (bounds 1.17e-2 – 2.97e-2).</li></ul><p>So the near-duplicate-row structure is <em>real and pervasive</em> in the pretrained backbone — every stage-2/3 attention and MLP projection carries rows within ~1e-2–3e-2 elementwise of an earlier representative.</p><h2 id="why-nothing-delivered-—-extraction-economics-not-the-gate" tabindex="-1">Why nothing delivered — extraction economics, not the gate <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-nothing-delivered-—-extraction-economics-not-the-gate" aria-label="Permalink to “Why nothing delivered — extraction economics, not the gate”">​</a></h2><p><code>n_bounds_delivered == 0</code> at every budget and <strong>no block was declined by the bound gate</strong> (no <code>accepted bound</code> error strings) — the bounded members simply were never the cheapest extracted term. Measured directly on <code>bb.layers.2.blocks.0.attn.qkv</code> (search → <code>alternatives</code>):</p><ul><li>dense <code>linear(input, W, b)</code> — 832 773 backend-priced FLOPs;</li><li><code>elide_bounded</code> member — <code>index_select</code>-re-expanded shrunk weight <code>p_weight__bl2286x768</code> + bias add — <strong>1 099 421</strong>.</li></ul><p>Only <strong>18 of 2304 rows</strong> dedup at ≤9.9e-3 (0.78% of the weight), so the gather/re-expand/bias-add wrapper costs 1.32× the dense member. Stories15M paid because its tied 32 000-row head is <em>dominated</em> by near-duplicates; BiRefNet&#39;s structure is real but ~0.8% thin — launch-aware pricing refuses it, correctly.</p><p>fp32 gate: every delivered module is bitwise identical to source (<code>fresh_rel = 0.0</code>, e2e <code>max_abs_diff = 0.0</code>) — the extracted terms were all exact members. Held-out mask proxy: <code>max_abs = 0</code>, IoU = 1.0 on all cells.</p><h2 id="verdict" tabindex="-1">Verdict <a class="header-anchor" href="#verdict" aria-label="Permalink to “Verdict”">​</a></h2><p><strong>The precision lane does not pay on BiRefNet — not because the bound machinery failed, but because the real structure is too thin to price.</strong> The bounded offers exist (20–56 sites, certified bounds inside budget), the bound gate stayed honest, and the cost model correctly declined a 1.32×-more-expensive member. Combined with the earlier trial&#39;s FLOP-neutral exact lane, neither mlopt lane buys headroom on this architecture; <code>torch.compile</code> alone (1.16×) is the bigger lever. New findings beyond the budget question: <code>deform_conv2d</code> has no lowering binding (a real coverage gap — export emits it, the sink can&#39;t lower it), compositional grafting is unsafe for modules called at multiple resolutions (e2e reports but doesn&#39;t roll back), and <code>capture_inputs</code>&#39; retained clones scale to ~6 GB at 220 M params — survivable only via filtered block selection on this host.</p>`,25)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};